---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/biology/foundational/biological-molecules">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    A living cell is a factory with an absurdly small inventory: four families of molecule
    supply every structure, every fuel and every instruction. You can tell the families
    apart by their elements: <strong>carbohydrates</strong> and <strong>fats</strong> contain
    carbon, hydrogen and oxygen; <strong>proteins</strong> add nitrogen — and often sulfur —
    to that list; and the fourth family, <strong>DNA</strong>, adds phosphorus. Each family
    is assembled from small repeating units: <strong>starch, glycogen and cellulose</strong>
    are all chains of <strong>glucose</strong> — the same brick, three different buildings;
    <strong>proteins</strong> are chains of <strong>amino acids</strong> (twenty-odd kinds,
    strung in any order); <strong>fats and oils</strong> are built from
    <strong>fatty acids and glycerol</strong>; DNA is built from <strong>nucleotides</strong>.
    Big molecules from small ones, joined and split again by enzymes — that is the whole
    economy of the cell, and it means biology can be read as chemistry with a supply chain.
  </p>
  <p>
    Why should you care which family a molecule belongs to? Because the answer predicts its
    job. Fast fuel is carbohydrate; long-term storage is fat; machinery is protein; the
    archive is DNA. A food label, a diet, a pathology report — all of them are statements
    about these four families, in different fonts.
  </p>

  <h2>Carbohydrates: sugars, starches and the three glucose buildings</h2>
  <p>
    Carbohydrates are sugars and their polymers, with the general formula roughly
    C<sub>x</sub>(H₂O)<sub>y</sub> — "hydrated carbon". The <strong>monosaccharides</strong>
    (single sugars — glucose, fructose) are the transportable fuel: glucose is what
    respiration burns and what blood carries. Two units joined make
    <strong>disaccharides</strong> (sucrose = glucose + fructose, the sugar plants ship in
    sap; lactose = glucose + galactose in milk). Long chains are
    <strong>polysaccharides</strong>, and the three glucose polymers repay a closer look,
    because identical bricks can build three utterly different buildings. <strong>Starch</strong>
    is the compact, slightly branched store plants fill their potatoes and seeds with.
    <strong>Glycogen</strong> is the animal version — more heavily branched, so glucose can
    be snapped off quickly from many ends in your liver and muscles: your glycogen store is
    about 400–500 g, enough fuel for a marathon's halfway mark. <strong>Cellulose</strong>
    uses a different linkage between the same glucose units — the bond has a different
    geometry (β-linkage, which human enzymes cannot break) — producing straight chains that
    hydrogen-bond into cables: the fibre in cell walls that we cannot digest but trees stand
    on. One molecule, three linkages, three fates: instant fuel, medium-term store,
    structural scaffolding.
  </p>

  <h2>Proteins: the machinery family</h2>
  <p>
    Proteins are chains of <strong>amino acids</strong> — small molecules sharing a common
    backbone but each carrying a different side group, twenty standard kinds. String fifty
    of them in any order and you have more possible sequences than there are atoms in the
    universe, which is how protein does so many jobs. The chain
    (<strong>primary structure</strong>) folds: hydrogen bonds coil it into helices and
    sheets (<strong>secondary</strong>), the side groups pack the whole into a precise 3-D
    shape (<strong>tertiary</strong>), and separate chains can dock together
    (<strong>quaternary</strong> — haemoglobin is four chains). The shape <em>is</em> the
    function. Enzymes are proteins folded into an <strong>active site</strong> whose shape
    fits one substrate — catalysing reactions a millionfold faster and staying unchanged;
    keratin and collagen are proteins folded into fibres (hair, tendon); haemoglobin folds
    into a gripper that carries oxygen; insulin is a peptide hormone; antibodies are
    proteins folded to recognise shapes they have never met. Heat or wrong pH disrupt the
    hydrogen bonds and the shape unravels — <strong>denaturation</strong> — which is why a
    boiled egg never unboils and why a fever above ~41 °C is dangerous: your enzymes are
    approaching their shape limits. Digestion, meanwhile, is protein-catalysed chain-cutting:
    proteases snip proteins into amino acids, amylase starch into maltose, lipase fats into
    fatty acids and glycerol — big insoluble polymers into small soluble monomers, the
    universal job of digestion.
  </p>

  <h2>Lipids: the dense fuel and the wall material</h2>
  <p>
    Fats and oils are built from <strong>fatty acids and glycerol</strong>: one glycerol
    backbone esterified with three fatty acids (a <em>triglyceride</em>). The families'
    chemistry explains their roles. Fat stores about twice the energy per gram as
    carbohydrate (roughly 39 kJ/g against 17 kJ/g) — because its carbon is already
    hydrogen-rich and it is stored <em>anhydrous</em> (1 g of glycogen drags ~3 g of bound
    water with it; fat does not) — which is why seeds pack oil, migrating birds fuel on fat,
    and your body banks fat rather than starch for lean times. <strong>Saturated</strong>
    fatty acids have no carbon–carbon double bonds: straight chains that pack tightly, hence
    solid butter and lard, and the association with raising blood cholesterol.
    <strong>Unsaturated</strong> fats (olive oil, fish oils) have kinks at their double
    bonds, stay liquid, and are the "healthy" side of the label. Phospholipids — the same
    triglyceride with one tail swapped for a phosphate head — self-assemble into the
    double-layer sheets that every cell membrane is made of: the movement-into-cells lesson
    lives inside this molecule. (And vitamins A, D, E and K are fat-soluble — the reason
    salads dressed with a little oil actually deliver their vitamins.)
  </p>

  <h2>Water: the solvent everything happens in</h2>
  <p>
    None of this chemistry runs on dry molecules. Water makes up ~70% of cell mass and is
    the medium of digestion, excretion and transport, and it earns the job by dissolving
    extraordinary amounts of solutes — because its molecules are polar and pry apart ionic
    and polar substances. Sucrose — the sugar plants ship around in phloem sap and we stir
    into tea — is one of the most soluble common solids known. Handbook data:
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="solubility of sucrose in water against temperature (CRC Handbook data) — hot water dissolves nearly three times more sugar than cold" widget="plotter" config={solubilityCfg} />

  <p>
    Trace the roles through one meal. <strong>Digestion</strong>: starch, proteins and fats
    are insoluble polymers, so enzymes must first break them into soluble glucose, amino
    acids and fatty acids — digestion is the chemistry of making food water-soluble.
    <strong>Transport</strong>: blood plasma carries glucose, amino acids, ions and urea in
    solution, and phloem sap ships sucrose the same way (at ~1 mol/dm³ — you could not stir
    that in a kitchen at room temperature; the graph shows why plants push it warmer of
    necessity in some climates). <strong>Excretion</strong>: urea leaves the body dissolved
    in water — no solvent, no exit. Water's other talent, crossing partially permeable
    membranes by osmosis, is the subject of its own lesson.
  </p>

  <h2>How to identify them: the chemical tests</h2>
  <p>
    You cannot see molecules, but you can test for them. These are the five tests the
    syllabus requires — each with its positive result, and each a classic practical:
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="five tests, five colour changes — the working detection kit of food science" widget="equationStep" config={testsCfg} />

  <p>
    A note on honesty in practicals: Benedict's test needs <em>heating</em> (a water bath at
    about 80 °C, not a naked flame on the tube), the biuret reagent is added drop by drop to
    avoid a false precipitate, and the ethanol emulsion test works only if the sample
    actually dissolves in the ethanol first. Negative results are still results — distilled
    water should fail all five, which is exactly why you run it as a control. Worked
    example of the kit in action: a "sports bar" that tests blue-black with iodine
    (starch), brick-red with Benedict's after heating (reducing sugars), lilac with biuret
    (protein) and cloudy with the ethanol emulsion test (fat) has declared its contents to
    you more reliably than the marketing on the wrapper.
  </p>

  <h2>DNA: the molecule that instructs the others</h2>
  <p>
    One more molecule belongs here, because it outranks the other three:
    <strong>DNA</strong> holds the instructions for building every protein above. It is
    built from <strong>nucleotide</strong> units (a sugar, a phosphate, and one of four
    bases), but its shape is what matters: <strong>two strands coiled into a double
    helix</strong>, each strand carrying a sequence of <strong>bases</strong>, with hydrogen
    <strong>bonds between paired bases</strong> holding the strands together. Pairing is
    strict: <strong>A pairs with T, and C pairs with G</strong>. That one rule makes the
    molecule endlessly copyable (each strand templates its partner) and endlessly readable —
    the order of bases spells, three at a time, the order of amino acids in every protein.
    Notice the division of labour it creates: DNA is the archive, proteins are the workforce,
    and the carbohydrate and lipid families are the payroll and the building materials.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The deeper structure is that all four families are <strong>polymers built by the same
    reaction</strong>: a <em>condensation</em> reaction joins units and releases water (the
    bond between two glucose units, two amino acids — the peptide bond — or glycerol and a
    fatty acid), and <em>hydrolysis</em> reverses it, adding water to split the bond. Digestion,
    then, is hydrolysis at industrial scale, and dehydration synthesis is why your body
    needs water to build as well as to dissolve. The energy economics fall out of the same
    chemistry: carbohydrates are partially oxidised already (hence 17 kJ/g), fats are
    hydrogen-rich and reduce fully (39 kJ/g), and protein sits between (17 kJ/g usable) —
    the numbers on every dietitian's chart are combustion energies, measured in a bomb
    calorimeter a century before biochemistry could explain them.
  </p>
  <p>
    Three expert edges. First, <strong>enzymes as molecular recognition</strong>: the
    lock-and-key picture is really weak-force complementarity — dozens of simultaneous
    hydrogen bonds and electrostatic grips, which is why an enzyme's rate collapses when pH
    shifts protonation by a fraction. Second, <strong>the misfold frontier</strong>: the
    same twenty bricks can build both health and prion disease, where a protein in the
    wrong shape templates its shape onto others (mad-cow disease, Alzheimer's plaques) —
    shape, not composition, is the disease variable. Third, <strong>lipid biology beyond
    storage</strong>: cell membranes are fluid mosaics whose stiffness is tuned by
    cholesterol and tail saturation (cold-adapted fish make more unsaturated tails to stay
    flexible), and signalling molecules (prostaglandins, steroid hormones) are lipids with
    messages on them. The four-family inventory, it turns out, is not a simplification — it
    is the actual parts list, and every branch of medicine is annotated in it.
  </p>
  <p>
    One worked enzyme practical shows the machinery measured, not just named. Amylase
    digests starch to maltose; starch tests blue-black with iodine, maltose does not.
    Mix amylase with starch suspension, take a drop every 30 seconds into iodine, and
    time the first drop that stays brown — that is the digestion time. Now vary one
    factor per experiment: at 10 °C the endpoint takes many minutes; at 37 °C it is
    fastest; at 60 °C it never comes (the enzyme has denatured); at pH 4 it crawls while
    at pH 7 it sprints (salivary amylase's optimum). The time-to-endpoint is the inverse
    rate, and the curves of rate against temperature and pH are the classic bell-and-
    cliff shapes: a gentle rise as molecules move faster, a hard collapse when the shape
    denatures. Enzyme kinetics, in other words, is measurable with iodine, a water bath
    and a stopwatch — the same experiment in miniature that biochemistry departments run
    with spectrophotometers.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Cambridge IGCSE Biology 0610 syllabus (2026–2028), unit 4; sucrose solubility data from CRC Handbook of Chemistry and Physics; OpenStax Biology 2e, ch. 3 (Biological Macromolecules); Campbell Biology (Urry et al.), 12th ed., ch. 4–5.</p>
</TopicPage>
